{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n11 \n \n \nHowever, only the larger gas-jet burner was used during the spaceflight tests.  The burners are \naddressed in detail in section 3.C of the ACME Engineering Implementation report. \n \nUnfortunately, the spherical burners did not always yield spherically symmetric flames, \npresumably because of (1) greater burner flow through the joint between the porous sphere and \nsupply tube, and (2) heat loss to the support tube (encompassing the supply tube) causing local \nflame quenching. \n \nTwo types of igniter tips have been used during ACME testing.  Most tests have been conducted \nusing a loop igniter tip like those which had been successfully used in the droplet combustion \nexperiments which preceded ACME research in the CIR.  However, some tests were conducted with \na coil igniter tip, where that included the CFI-G investigation and the second-round testing for \nFlame Design, Burning Rate Emulator (BRE), and Structure and Response of Spherical Diffusion \nFlames (s-Flame), with the exception of the latter\u2019s first day testing.  For more information about \nthe igniter tips, see section 3.D of the ACME Engineering Implementation report. \n \nThe chamber insert includes three mass flow controllers (visible on the left of Fig. 2) for the \ndelivery of fuel, oxidizer or inert (e.g., helium or carbon dioxide), and nitrogen to the burner.  The \nmaximum fuel flow is 2 slpm (on a nitrogen basis), whereas much higher flows, e.g., 10 slpm (on \na nitrogen basis), are possible for the oxidizer and nitrogen lines.  The crew can exchange the mass \nflow controllers to provide appropriate ranges that maximize the flow accuracy.  The gas delivery \nsystem enables on-orbit dilution of the fuel or oxidizer (but not both simultaneously), coflow, \ninverse flames (e.g., oxidizer delivery to the center of a coflow burner), and premixed flames, \nalthough the latter are not planned for any of the current ACME experiments.  Gases can also be \ndelivered premixed to the space station where that is necessary in some circumstances, such as \nthe blending of different fuel gases.  CIR\u2019s standard bottle sizes are 1, 2.25, and 3.8 liters but ACME \nwill sometimes use smaller bottles as a hazard control to limit the deficient reactant (e.g., fuel) \nthat can be released into the combustion chamber. \n \nACME tests were not conducted with carbon dioxide as a diluent.  Furthermore, the fuel diluted", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 11}}